# Line-ending policy for the codelisperer monorepo.
#
# Every text file is LF in the repo AND in every working tree, on every OS.
# SBCL compiles the working-tree files directly, and a CRLF turns a FORMAT
# ~<newline> continuation into an illegal ~<Return> directive -- surfaced as a
# compile-time "macroexpansion" error. Forcing LF on checkout makes the build
# identical on Linux/macOS/Windows and immunizes against core.autocrlf.
#
# PLANNED, not true today (#387): source was meant to ALSO avoid FORMAT ~<newline>
# continuations entirely, so that a stray CRLF from a zip download or a misconfigured
# editor -- a tree that did not arrive through `git checkout', where the rule above
# cannot reach -- could not break the build either. There are 53, across 13 files.
# CLAUDE.md and AGENTS.md state the rule for new code and it holds for new code; the
# existing 53 are a sweep nobody has done. Severity is low precisely because the line
# below does hold: every git checkout is LF on every OS, and nothing is broken today.
# #387 carries the sweep and the checker that would keep it swept, in that order --
# a checker landing first would need a baseline of known violations, and a stored
# count of those is an allowlist wearing a number.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts are the deliberate exception -- they want CRLF.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binary assets: never normalize.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.exe   binary
*.dll   binary
*.dylib binary
*.so    binary
*.gz    binary
*.zip   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
